  "summary": "Large and elaborate English country houses built by wealthy Elizabethan and Jacobean aristocrats to display social status and power.",
  "description": "Prodigy houses emerged during England's economic expansion when wealthy landowners built extravagant homes with symmetrical facades, elaborate plasterwork, and extensive gardens to demonstrate their wealth and social standing. They represented a unique English interpretation of Renaissance principles blended with medieval traditions.",
  "culturalContext": "Prodigy houses — the enormous show-palaces of Elizabethan and Jacobean England (Longleat, Burghley, Hardwick Hall, Hatfield) — were built by courtier-competitors between the 1550s and 1620s to lodge the monarch's progress and broadcast new money. Symmetric E-plans, enormous glass walls (\"Hardwick Hall, more glass than wall\"), and Renaissance detail by master masons like Robert Smythson made them England's first architect-led country houses.",
